+ podgreatpersons.0259.desc: "The daughter of a painter and poet, Ben no Naishi rose to the imperial court with grace. She served Emperor Go-Fukakusa as naishi-no-kami, entrusted with sacred rites and the divine regalia itself. She has recorded palace life in skilled brushstrokes, offering many a window into the royal world. More than a scribe, she was a counselor, a keeper of beauty.\nHowever, quite abruptly, she seems to have disappeared from court after the emperor's abdication. Some say she has chosen a quiet retirement, others think of darker possibilities. Whatever the truth may be, finding this court lady should be possible, if you pulled some strings."
+ podgreatpersons.0260.t: "Lohgawe"
+ podgreatpersons.0260.desc: "Lohgawe is an Indian brahmin, unparalleled in virtue and spiritual matters. He traveled far from India to the island of Java, seeking the incarnation of Vishnu.\nHe mentored the young outcast Ken Arok, turning him from a cunning thief leader into a king. He also prophecized to Ken Arok that Ken Dedes, the wife of his employer, would give birth to kings but advised Ken Arok to stay away from her.\nIgnoring his mentor's advice, and infatuated with Ken Dedes' beauty, Ken Arok killed his employer, took Ken Dedes as his wife, and would go on to rule the Kingdom of Singhasari.\nNow weak and frail, you can easily stage Lohgawe's disappearance and claim him as your own."
+ podgreatpersons.0261.t: "Ken Dedes"
+ podgreatpersons.0261.desc: "Ken Dedes was born from a humble Hindu priest. When the local ruler, Tunggul Ametung, fell in love with her, kidnapped and married her, Ken Dedes waited for an opportunity to enact her revenge. That opportunity took the form of Ken Arok, Tunggul's bodyguard. Seducing him, she then lead Ken Arok to plan the murder of her kidnapper.\nThe child she got from her first marriage, Anusapati, having learned the truth about his father and his demise, later went on the path of revenge and murdered Ken Arok, only to be killed by Ken Dedes' other son, Tohjava.\nSupposedly raised with Buddhist virtues, you can only wonder if those murders are all due to her machinations. In any case, with most of her sons killing each other, and out of the eyes of the court, you could claim the first queen of Singharasi, and hope that she doesn't sire someone who will eventually replace you."
+ podgreatpersons.0262.t: "Mahisa Anabrang"
+ podgreatpersons.0262.desc: "Mahisa Anabrang is the admiral that led the Singhasarian expedition into the Pamalayu kingdom. After 18 years of warring abroad, he came back with two Pamalayuan princesses to be wed to his king. However, Kertanegara, his king, had been killed in the Jayakatwang rebellion the previous year. Anabrang decided to swear loyalty to Raden Wijawa, one of Kertanegara's relatives, who vanquished the rebels and created the Majapahit Kingdom soon after. To secure his position further, the king took the princesses that Anabrang brought back with him as wives.\nThe Majapahit's first rebellion has since started, and the position of Mahisa Anabrang is dire, being the latest loyal follower of Raden Wijawa, stuck near the Tamba Besas river.\nIf you want to snatch the loyal admiral to $pod_Embrace$ him, you should move sooner than later."
+ podgreatpersons.0263.t: "Dyah Halayudah"
+ podgreatpersons.0263.desc: "Jealous, treacherous and ambitious, Dyay Halayudah was denied the position of [Glossary('Rakryan patih', 'podgloss.rakryan_patih')] in favor of lesser born people. Her machinations led to the fall of the three heroes of the Majapahit: Rangga Lawe rebelled and was killed by Mahisa Anabrang, Lembu Sora was exiled, and she led the king to believe that Nambi, the first [Glossary('Rakryan patih', 'podgloss.rakryan_patih')], rebelled against Jayanagara, the king, to get him killed.\nJayanagara, the king of Majapahit, went along with these machinations as he was immoral and prone to scheming himself, and Mahapati's schemes helped seized back the eastern half of Majapahit from Wirarja eastward.\nHowever, now that Majapahit is firmly secure in Jayanagara's hands, her crimes are being exposed one by one. Sentenced to a death by a thousand cuts, you could snatch Mahapati for yourself and replace her with a decoy."
+ podgreatpersons.0264.t: "Citra Rashmi"
+ podgreatpersons.0264.desc: "When Hayam Wuruk became the emperor of Majapahit, he decided to marry the beautiful Sundanese princess Citra Rashmi, to secure an alliance with his westward neighbor. When the royal family arrived at Bubat square in Trowulan, to celebrate the marriage and alliance between the two kingdoms, Gajah Mada, the [Glossary('Mahapatih','podgloss.maha_patih')] of Majapahit wished to establish dominance of Majapahit over Sunda and asked for Citra Rashmi to be no more than a concubine.\nThe proposition did not sit well with the royal family and they refused, preparing to defend their honour. Majapahit troops outnumber the royal entourgae of Sunda by a ridiculous amount and what will come is not a battle, but a slaughter.\nCitra Rashmi, known as Pitaloka, as well as most of the women from the royal family will probably kill herself, as the ethos of the [Glossary('kshatriya','podgloss.kshatriya')] dictates, to avoid being dishonored.\nThere's not much time left to save Pitaloka from her fate, but you could switch out the princess for a decoy."
+ podgreatpersons.0265.t: "Gajah Mada"
+ podgreatpersons.0265.desc: "Starting as a [Glossary('Bhayangkara', 'podgloss.bhayangkara')] commander, and saving the life of Jayanegara, Gajah Mada rose to the position of [Glossary('Mahapatih', 'podgloss.maha_patih')] after crushing rebellions and helped the queen Tribhuwana to secure the Majahapit empire.\nDuring his nomination he took the [Glossary('Sumpah Palapa', 'podgloss.sumpah_palapa')] to expand the empire until it encompassed the entirety of the Nusantara archipelago.\nHis ambition, however, will be his downfall. The death of Pitaloka and her family during the battle of Bubat led Hayam Wuruk to push him to retirement, and his position of Mahapatih was split among four different Patih.\nForgotten by many, the last Mahapatih now awaits death in his estate, and it would be easy for you to add him to your entourage."
+ podgreatpersons.0266.t: "Urduja"
+ podgreatpersons.0266.desc: "The kingdom of Talawasi is a strange one. Home of the [Glossary('Kinalakian', 'podgloss.kinalakian')], it is a matriarchal society led by warrior princesses. One of them, Urduja, has made the vow to only marry a man who could best her in single combat and is, as of now, still unwed and childless, most men being terrified by her martial skills.\nThe Talawasi sense of hospitality is, however, extended to any merchant or travellers that come across the northern islands of Nusantara, as can attest Ibn Battuta, the navigator who visited all muslim lands, and spoke of the banquets and prosperity of Talawasi.\nUrduja's days are coming to an end though, her plans of an invasion of the pepper island, what you know as India, can't possibly end well for the warrior princess.\nAn impromptu accident could be arranged if you were to recruit her, leaving the Kinalakian kingdom in the hands of her sister."
+ podgreatpersons.0267.t: "Tantular"
+ podgreatpersons.0267.desc: "Mpu Tantular is a poet, theologian and philosopher and nephew of Hayam Wuruk, the emperor of Majahapit. Unwavering in his buddhist beliefs, Tantular is known for his tolerance and appreciation of other beliefs, and dedicated his writings to them.\nHis most famous work, however, remains the [Glossary('Kakawin Sutasoma','podgloss.kakawin')], from which the verse [Glossary('Bhinneka Tunggal Ika', 'podgloss.bhinneka_tunggal_ika')] comes from, and which glorifies the cultural and religious diversity of the Majahapit empire.\nWhile never holding an official role in the imperial court, he is a teacher and a personal advisor of Hayam Wuruk, using his philosophical insight to guide Majahapit through the indianisation of the archipelago.\nNow that the emperor is dead, making him disappear and join your court for an $pod_Embrace$ is relatively straightforward."
+ podgreatpersons.0268.t: "Sang Adi Putra"
+ podgreatpersons.0268.desc: "Sang Adi Putra, a master of [Glossary('Silat', 'podgloss.silat')], was meditating in a cave in Mount Ledang. When the admiral Hang Tuah from the Malay sultanate of Malacca came to find him, he agreed to delay his personal quest of introspection to train him and his companions. Once their training was deemed sufficient by their master, they came back to Malacca and, to the surprise of the [Glossary('Bentahara', 'podgloss.bentahara')], managed to fight back the rebels and bandits that were plaguing the region.\nAfter having been disrupted in his meditation, Sang Adi Putra returned to the cave in the mountain to meditate.\nYour agents found the mythical place he's resting in and you could try and subjugate him to $pod_Embrace$ him."
+ podgreatpersons.0269.t: "Sayyid Ali Rahmatullah"
+ podgreatpersons.0269.desc: "Born in Champa, Sayyid Ali Rahmatullah moved to Java when his aunt married Kertawijawa, king of Majapahit. From there, he started spreading Islam, and has been the teacher of Raden Patah. Raden Patah has, since then, founded the Demak Sultanate, one of the first Sultanates of the archipelago.\nA descendant of Muhammad, the Prophet, Sayyid Ali Rahmatullah has become one of the [Glossary('Wali Songo', 'podgloss.wally_songo')] and earned the name of Sunan Ampel for his efforts in spreading Islam and helping wth the consolidation of the Sultanate. His effort led to the construction of the Demak Great Mosque, and now that the work on this remarkable mosque is done, Susan Ampel has retired, letting his sons taking over his life's work.\nThe old sage now peacefully awaits death, and an intervention on your side could lead you to $pod_Embrace$ him."
+ podgreatpersons.0270.t: "Shi Da-jie"
+ podgreatpersons.0270.desc: "The daughter of Shi Jinqing, who helped Zheng He defeat the pirates of Palembang, lost control of her family assets when her father died to the benefit of her sister. Forced out of Palembang, Shi Da-jie moved to Java, and was appointed Gresik's [Glossary('shahbander','podgloss.shahbander')] by the Majapahit ruler. Focused on rebuilding a power base, she developed Gresik's ports and trade and turned it into one of the biggest trade ports of the area, sending ships to Bali, the Moluccas and Cambodia.\nTo facilitate trade with the sultanates, she encouraged the spread of Islam in the archipelago, and raised her children according to the principles of Islam. One of them, Sunan Giri, will become instrumental in the large scale conversion of the archipelago.\nNow retired, the Great Lady of Gresik enjoys the fruits of her life of labor in her estate. If you want to $pod_Embrace$ her, now is the time to move."
+ podgreatpersons.0271.t: "Tun Perak"
+ podgreatpersons.0271.desc: "Starting his carreer as a very effective governor for the Malaccan Sultanate, Tun Perak was instrumental in the defeat of the Siamese during their invasion of Malacca. He did so, notably, by allowing women in the army, to motivate the men to fight harder. His victory over the Siamese led the Sultan to nominate him [Glossary('Bentahara', 'podgloss.bentahara')].\nLoyal to a fault to the sultanate, he helped secure the realm and expanded it under four different sultans, and when the sultan's son murdered his own son, he avoided seeking revenge and led the Sultanate to its apogee.\nHis health is declining and his brother is now stepping in. Organizing his disappearance from the public eye would allow for you to $pod_Embrace$ him."
+ podgreatpersons.0272.t: "Tun Fatimah"
+ podgreatpersons.0272.desc: "Daughter of the Malaccan [Glossary('Bentahara', 'podgloss.bentahara')], Fatimah learned how to fight from an early age. When the Portuguese arrived in the harbor for the first time, Fatimah, dressed as a man, and her brothers fought fiercely and pushed the traders and colonizers back to the sea. Impressed by both her martial skills and her beauty, the Sultan wished to marry her. Her father refused, for he had other plans for her.\nOut of rage, the Sultan murdered her entire family and forced her into a marriage. Trying to avenge her family, Fatimah never smiled to the sultan, and aborted at least three times before she managed to secure her son as the first in line in succession.\nHowever, her plans would not have the time to bear fruit, as the Portuguese came back. And, this time, fearing Tun Fatimah, they overwhelmed the Malaccan fleet and pushed the Sultan and his suite into exile.\nNow powerless and on the run, you can give Tun Fatimah the power she always wanted by securing an $pod_Embrace$."
+ podgreatpersons.0273.t: "Laksamana Malahayati"
+ podgreatpersons.0273.desc: "The daughter of a [Glossary('laksamana','podgloss.laksamana')], Malahayati was raised with a martial education and led the royal guards of the Aceh Sultanate. When the dutch delegation, led by Cornelis de Houtman, insulted the Sultan, it was Malahayati and her army of widows that managed to fight the Dutch and kill the offender. Maintaining a strong hold on the Aceh coast, she forced out the European traders and kept the straits open only to Muslim and Asian boats.\nWhen the kingdom of England wanted to open trade in the area, they decided to cut their losses and send an emissary to the Sultan to peacefully negotiate an agreement. Malahayati was the negotiator the Sultan chose, as he refused to trust any men with such a matter.\nAlas, the Portuguese do not have the diplomatic touch of England and they're now surrounding Malahayati's fleet.\nIf you want to $pod_Embrace$ her, you will have to send your agent and kidnap her before her inevitable demise."
+ podgreatpersons.0274.t: "Zhao Rukuo"
+ podgreatpersons.0274.desc: "Zhao Rukuo is a legend within the Middle Kingdom and beyond. Born into a far-off branch of the Song Imperial Clan, he has risen through the ranks to eventually become a Supervisor of Maritime Trade.\nHis greatest achievement is the Zhu Fan Zhu. The two-tome work contains an encyclopedic description of the many cultures, places, and peoples known to the Middle Kingdom. From Indonesia to the Steppes, India to Arabia, and even beyond, the book details all.\nHowever, this great man’s life draws to a close, he is sick and will likely not recover. Though perhaps this might not be the end of his existence. The first tome of Zhu Fan Zhu detailed culture and the second, goods.\n$pod_Embrace$ him, and perhaps a third tome shall detail the supernatural."
